  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable through the weekend. \n\nHigh pressure passing over the Great Lakes will maintain fair \nweather through the weekend. Clear skies and light winds will \nsupport a seasonably cool night tonight with a low chance for frost \ninland from the lakes. High pressure will slide off into New England \nby Sunday. Southerly flow on the western flank of the departing \nridge will encourage a modest warming trend across the region with \nhigh temperatures in the mid to upper 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures with frost potential \nearly next week.\n\nA potent shortwave trough is forecast to track across eastern Canada \nand the Northeast Sunday night and Monday. Meanwhile, a sharp cold\nfront will slide across the region Sunday night. Moisture fields\nare not overly impressive, but decent frontal support should \nbring at least a broken line of showers across the North Country.\nThe remainder of the area should remain dry with only the outside\nchance for a shower with the frontal passage. \n\nA cooler airmass will settle in behind the front with 850 mb \ntemperatures dropping just below zero C (-1C to -3C) by Monday \nevening. Given the chilly airmass aloft moving across the warm \nlake waters and some limited remaining shallow moisture it is \nlikely we will see some lake induced clouds and maybe even a few\nshowers. The main story though will be the early fall chill in \nthe air on Monday, with afternoon highs only expected in the \nupper 40s to 50s. \n\nSurface high pressure moving nearly overhead Monday night will \nallow temperatures to bottom out in the 30s, with some upper \n20s possible in the coldest inland locations. A widespread frost\nlooks likely during this period.",
